<PaperLayout
  title="Improving the Language Understanding Capabilities of Large Language Models Using Reinforcement Learning"
  eyebrow="LLM · RL for NLU · 2025 / arXiv"
  authors="Sai Ashish Somayajula · Bokai Hu · Qi Cao · Xin Pan · Pengtao Xie"
  arxivUrl="https://arxiv.org/html/2410.11020v5"
  subtitle="A structured reading of a paper that reframes natural language understanding as a reinforcement-learning problem for instruction-tuned LLMs."
  sections={sections}
  annotations={annotations}
  initialAnnotation="nlu"
  compactTitle={true}
>
        <section id="overview" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Overview</h2>

          <div class="guide-box">
            <strong>Reading guide</strong>
            <p>
              Read this paper as a paper about <em>task alignment</em>, not as a paper about a new model architecture.
              The authors do not invent a new Transformer. Instead, they ask whether an instruction-tuned LLM can be made better at
              <button class="anno" data-anno="nlu">natural language understanding</button> by training it with
              task rewards rather than only with supervised answer imitation.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The paper starts from a surprising observation: smaller encoder-only models such as BERT can still beat
            7B-scale instruction-tuned LLMs on classic NLU benchmarks. A model like LLAMA2-7B-chat-hf can follow
            instructions and generate fluent text, but when it is asked to solve GLUE or SuperGLUE tasks with only
            prompting, it often produces unstable, biased, or poorly formatted answers.
          </p>

          <p>
            The authors' solution is to turn NLU into a reinforcement-learning environment. The model receives a
            prompt and generates an answer token by token. The generated answer is extracted from a fixed format,
            compared with the ground-truth label, and converted into a scalar
            <button class="anno" data-anno="reward">reward</button>. Then
            <button class="anno" data-anno="ppo">PPO</button> updates the model so that answer paths receiving
            higher reward become more likely.
          </p>

          <div class="architecture-flow history-flow">
            <span>Prompt + query</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>LLM generates answer</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Extract judgment</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Reward</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>PPO updates LoRA</span>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="problem" class="reading-section">
          <h2>The problem</h2>

          <p>
            The paper is built around a distinction that is easy to miss: <strong>generation ability is not the same as
            benchmarked understanding ability</strong>. Decoder-style LLMs are trained to continue text. GLUE and
            SuperGLUE often ask for precise judgments: positive or negative, entailment or contradiction, acceptable
            or unacceptable, similarity score, correct option, answer span.
          </p>

          <p>
            A prompted LLM must solve these tasks indirectly through generation. It may answer in the wrong format,
            over-explain instead of selecting a label, or show a class preference. Encoder-only models such as BERT
            are structurally closer to the benchmark interface: encode the input, read a representation, output a label.
          </p>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">The paper's central gap</span>
            <p>
              Under-14B instruction-tuned LLMs have broad linguistic knowledge, but prompting does not reliably turn
              that knowledge into stable NLU decisions. The authors argue that direct reward optimization can align
              the model's generation behavior with the task metric more effectively than prompting or ordinary
              supervised fine-tuning.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            This is why the paper compares against both BERT and LLM baselines. BERT asks, "How strong is a model
            designed for understanding?" <button class="anno" data-anno="zeroShot">Zero-shot prompting</button>
            asks, "How far can the LLM get with only a task instruction?" <button class="anno" data-anno="fewShot">Few-shot prompting</button>
            asks, "How far can the LLM get when a few labeled examples are placed inside the prompt, but no weights
            are updated?" <button class="anno" data-anno="sft">SFT</button> asks, "Is ordinary supervised adaptation
            enough?" PPO asks, "What if we optimize the final task reward directly?"
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="background" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Background concepts</h2>

          <p>
            Before getting into PPO, it helps to separate two kinds of names that appear throughout the discussion:
            benchmark names, which tell us what kind of understanding ability is being tested, and reinforcement-learning
            terms, which tell us how the model is being updated.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Name</th>
                  <th>Role</th>
                  <th>What it is</th>
                  <th>Why it matters here</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {benchmarkRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.name}</td>
                    <td>{row.role}</td>
                    <td>{row.what}</td>
                    <td>{row.why}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The most important reinforcement-learning translation is simple: an LLM can be treated as a
            <button class="anno" data-anno="policy">policy</button>. In ordinary RL, a policy gives the probability of
            taking an action in a state. In language generation, the state is the current token prefix, and the action
            is the next token. PPO updates the model by changing how likely it is to generate certain tokens after
            certain prefixes.
          </p>

          <div class="concept-grid">
            <div class="concept-card">
              <h3>Policy-based RL</h3>
              <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
                <code>
                  p<sub>θ</sub>(a | s)
                </code>
              </div>
              <p>
                Policy-based methods directly optimize the action-selection rule above. This matches language models
                naturally because a decoder-only LLM already outputs a probability distribution over the next token.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="concept-card">
              <h3>Value-based RL</h3>
              <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
                <code>
                  Q(s, a)
                </code>
              </div>
              <p>
                Value-based methods learn a value function, then choose actions according to that value. This is less
                convenient for LLM generation because the action space is the whole vocabulary, and one answer is a long
                sequence of actions rather than a single decision.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="concept-card">
              <h3>Actor-critic</h3>
              <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
                <code>
                  V<sub>t</sub> = V(s<sub>t</sub>)
                </code>
              </div>
              <p>
                The actor is the language model that generates tokens. The
                <button class="anno" data-anno="critic">critic</button> estimates how much future reward is expected
                from the current partial answer. That estimate helps PPO decide whether a generated token was better
                or worse than expected.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="method" class="reading-section">
          <h2>The method</h2>

          <p>
            The method has two parallel fine-tuning routes. In SFT, the training sequence contains the prompt, the
            input, and the ground-truth answer. The model is trained by next-token prediction, so it learns to imitate
            the correct answer text. In PPO, the model first generates an answer, then the environment scores that answer.
            The model is updated according to the reward.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Setup</th>
                  <th>Do weights change?</th>
                  <th>Input form</th>
                  <th>Purpose</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {promptingRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.setup}</td>
                    <td>{row.weights}</td>
                    <td>{row.input}</td>
                    <td>{row.purpose}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The prompt format matters because the reward function needs an answer it can parse. The paper asks the
            model to place the final decision inside tags such as <code>&lt;Judgement&gt;...&lt;/Judgement&gt;</code>.
            This turns an open-ended generation problem into a scorable answer-selection problem.
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <h3>Example: SST-2 as RL</h3>
            <p><strong>Prompt:</strong> Determine whether the sentence is positive or negative. Return only the answer in <code>&lt;Judgement&gt;</code> tags.</p>
            <p><strong>Query:</strong> <code>This movie is wonderful.</code></p>
            <p><strong>Generated output:</strong> <code>&lt;Judgement&gt;Positive&lt;/Judgement&gt;</code></p>
            <p><strong>Reward:</strong> If the ground-truth label is positive, the response receives high reward. If the model outputs negative, it receives lower reward. If it fails to produce a parseable judgment, it receives the format penalty.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="ppo" class="reading-section">
          <h2>How PPO actually runs</h2>

          <p>
            PPO is easiest to understand if we separate two phases that are often mixed together in the formula:
            <strong>rollout</strong> and <strong>update</strong>. During rollout, the current model generates answers and
            records the tokens it sampled. During update, PPO reuses those recorded tokens to change the trainable parameters.
            The new policy is not created before training starts; it is created by gradient updates on responses generated by
            the previous policy.
          </p>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">The key mental model</span>
            <p>
              First, the model acts. Then the answer is scored. Then PPO asks: should the model become more or less likely
              to generate the same tokens in the same states next time?
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3>1. What is being compared?</h3>

          <p>
            There are three different model states that are easy to confuse. The current policy is the model being updated.
            The old policy is the snapshot that generated the current rollout batch. The reference model is a longer-term
            anchor used by the KL penalty, usually kept fixed.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Name</th>
                  <th>Formula</th>
                  <th>Does it change?</th>
                  <th>What it is used for</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Current policy</td>
                  <td>
                    <code>
                      π<sub>θ</sub>
                    </code>
                  </td>
                  <td>Yes</td>
                  <td>
                    The policy currently being optimized. In this setup, the main trainable part is the LoRA-adapted policy,
                    not all original LLM weights.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <button class="anno" data-anno="oldPolicy">Old policy</button>
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    <code>
                      π<sub>θ′</sub>
                    </code>
                  </td>
                  <td>Yes, after each rollout</td>
                  <td>
                    The snapshot that generated the current batch of responses. PPO compares the current policy against this
                    snapshot so one update does not move too far.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <button class="anno" data-anno="referenceModel">Reference model</button>
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    <code>
                      π<sub>ref</sub>
                    </code>
                  </td>
                  <td>Usually fixed</td>
                  <td>
                    The longer-term anchor used by the KL penalty. It prevents the fine-tuned model from drifting too far from
                    the original language model distribution.
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            This means <code>θ′</code> is not simply the original model forever. It is a moving snapshot. If
            <code>θ<sub>0</sub></code> generates the first rollout, then <code>θ′ = θ<sub>0</sub></code> for that update.
            After PPO produces <code>θ<sub>1</sub></code>, the next rollout is generated by <code>θ<sub>1</sub></code>,
            so the next old policy becomes <code>θ′ = θ<sub>1</sub></code>.
          </p>

          <div class="architecture-flow">
            <span>
              π<sub>θ₀</sub> generates batch
            </span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>
              old policy is π<sub>θ₀</sub>
            </span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>PPO updates current policy</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>
              π<sub>θ₁</sub>
            </span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>
              next rollout uses π<sub>θ₁</sub>
            </span>
          </div>

          <h3>2. Rollout: generate first, update later</h3>

          <p>
            During rollout, the model receives a task prompt and generates a response. For an NLU classification example,
            the input might ask the model to place its answer inside a fixed tag:
          </p>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">Example rollout</span>
            <p>
              <strong>Input:</strong> Sentence: <em>This movie is wonderful.</em>
              <br />
              <strong>Prompt ending:</strong> <code>&lt;Judgement&gt;</code>
              <br />
              <strong>Generated output:</strong> <code>Positive &lt;/Judgement&gt;</code>
              <br />
              <strong>Full answer:</strong> <code>&lt;Judgement&gt;Positive&lt;/Judgement&gt;</code>
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The rollout creates a trajectory. At each token position, the state is the current prefix and the action is the
            next generated token.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Time step</th>
                  <th>State</th>
                  <th>Action</th>
                  <th>Recorded during rollout</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <code>t = 1</code>
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    <code>
                      s<sub>1</sub> = prompt + &lt;Judgement&gt;
                    </code>
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    <code>
                      a<sub>1</sub> = Positive
                    </code>
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    <code>
                      log p<sub>θ′</sub>(a<sub>1</sub> | s<sub>1</sub>)
                    </code>
                    and
                    <code>
                      V(s<sub>1</sub>)
                    </code>
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <code>t = 2</code>
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    <code>
                      s<sub>2</sub> = prompt + &lt;Judgement&gt; Positive
                    </code>
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    <code>
                      a<sub>2</sub> = &lt;/Judgement&gt;
                    </code>
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    <code>
                      log p<sub>θ′</sub>(a<sub>2</sub> | s<sub>2</sub>)
                    </code>
                    and
                    <code>
                      V(s<sub>2</sub>)
                    </code>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            Nothing has been updated yet. The rollout only records what the old policy did: which tokens it sampled, how
            likely those tokens were under the old policy, and what value the critic assigned to each prefix.
          </p>

          <h3>3. Reward: score the completed answer</h3>

          <p>
            The reward comes after the full response has been generated. For classification tasks, the answer is extracted
            from the judgement tag and compared with the ground-truth label.
          </p>

          <div class="concept-grid">
            <div class="concept-card">
              <h3>Correct format, correct answer</h3>
              <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
                <code>
                  R = 1
                </code>
              </div>
              <p>
                The generated label matches the ground truth.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="concept-card">
              <h3>Correct format, wrong answer</h3>
              <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
                <code>
                  R = 0
                </code>
              </div>
              <p>
                The model produced a parseable label, but the label is wrong.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="concept-card">
              <h3>Wrong format</h3>
              <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
                <code>
                  R = −1
                </code>
              </div>
              <p>
                The output cannot be reliably parsed. This is the largest penalty for classification tasks.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For STS-B, the task is regression rather than classification, so the reward is based on distance from the true
            similarity score:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              R = 2.5 − |ŷ<sub>i</sub> − y<sub>i</sub>|
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            Since STS-B scores are between 0 and 5, the largest possible error is 5. The reward therefore ranges from
            <code>−2.5</code> to <code>2.5</code>. A formatting failure receives <code>−2.5</code>, the lowest possible reward.
          </p>

          <h3>4. Critic: estimate the value of each prefix</h3>

          <p>
            The <button class="anno" data-anno="critic">critic</button> does not predict the next token. It takes the hidden
            representation of a generated prefix and outputs one scalar value. That value means: from this partial answer,
            how much future reward should we expect?
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              V<sub>t</sub> = V(s<sub>t</sub>)
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            A common confusion is how the critic can use <code>V<sub>t+1</sub></code> if the model is currently at time
            <code>t</code>. The answer is that PPO computes these values after rollout. Once the full response has been
            generated, the sequence of prefixes already exists:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              s<sub>1</sub>, s<sub>2</sub>, ..., s<sub>T</sub>
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            So <code>V<sub>t+1</sub></code> is not a magical look into the future. It is the critic's value estimate for the
            next prefix that already appeared in the sampled trajectory.
          </p>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">Example</span>
            <p>
              If <code>s<sub>t</sub> = prompt + &lt;Judgement&gt;</code>, the critic might estimate
              <code>V(s<sub>t</sub>) = 0.35</code>. If the next generated token is <code>Positive</code>, then
              <code>s<sub>t+1</sub> = prompt + &lt;Judgement&gt; Positive</code>, and the critic might estimate
              <code>V(s<sub>t+1</sub>) = 0.80</code>. That means the prefix containing <code>Positive</code> looks more likely
              to lead to a high reward.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3>5. Advantage: assign credit backward</h3>

          <p>
            The reward usually arrives only after the full answer is finished. PPO still needs to know whether each earlier
            token helped or hurt. That is the role of the advantage estimate.
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              δ<sub>t</sub> = R<sub>t</sub> + γV<sub>t+1</sub> − V<sub>t</sub>
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            This is the temporal-difference error. It compares what the critic expected at the current state with what actually
            happened after taking the action and moving to the next state.
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              Â<sub>t</sub> = δ<sub>t</sub> + γλÂ<sub>t+1</sub>
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            This is Generalized Advantage Estimation. It is computed backward from the end of the generated response:
          </p>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">Backward computation</span>
            <p>
              Start with <code>Â<sub>T+1</sub> = 0</code>. Compute <code>Â<sub>T</sub></code>, then
              <code>Â<sub>T−1</sub></code>, then <code>Â<sub>T−2</sub></code>, and continue until
              <code>Â<sub>1</sub></code>. This is why the formula can depend on <code>Â<sub>t+1</sub></code> without causing
              a circular dependency.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Term</th>
                  <th>Meaning</th>
                  <th>Intuition</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <code>
                      R<sub>t</sub>
                    </code>
                  </td>
                  <td>Immediate reward</td>
                  <td>
                    Often zero for intermediate tokens and nonzero at the end of the response.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <code>
                      γ
                    </code>
                  </td>
                  <td>Discount factor</td>
                  <td>
                    Controls how much future value matters compared with immediate reward.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <code>
                      λ
                    </code>
                  </td>
                  <td>GAE smoothing parameter</td>
                  <td>
                    Controls the trade-off between short-term TD estimates and longer-range credit assignment.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <code>
                      V<sub>t</sub>
                    </code>
                  </td>
                  <td>Critic estimate at the current prefix</td>
                  <td>
                    What the critic expected before seeing the next token.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <code>
                      V<sub>t+1</sub>
                    </code>
                  </td>
                  <td>Critic estimate at the next prefix</td>
                  <td>
                    What the critic expects after the sampled token has been appended.
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h3>6. PPO update: change token probabilities carefully</h3>

          <p>
            Once advantages are computed, PPO updates the policy. It takes the same sampled state-action pairs from rollout
            and compares the current probability of each sampled token with the probability assigned by the old policy.
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              r<sub>t</sub>(θ) =
              p<sub>θ</sub>(a<sub>t</sub> | s<sub>t</sub>)
              /
              p<sub>θ′</sub>(a<sub>t</sub> | s<sub>t</sub>)
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            At the beginning of the update, <code>θ</code> and <code>θ′</code> are the same, so the ratio is close to
            <code>1</code>. As gradient updates change <code>θ</code>, the numerator changes, but the denominator stays fixed
            because it was recorded during rollout.
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              J(θ) = E[min(r<sub>t</sub>(θ)Â<sub>t</sub>,
              clip(r<sub>t</sub>(θ), 1−ε, 1+ε)Â<sub>t</sub>)]
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            If <code>Â<sub>t</sub> &gt; 0</code>, the sampled token was better than expected, so PPO tries to increase its
            probability. If <code>Â<sub>t</sub> &lt; 0</code>, the token was worse than expected, so PPO tries to decrease its
            probability. The clipping term prevents either change from becoming too large in one update.
          </p>

          <div class="concept-grid">
            <div class="concept-card">
              <h3>Positive advantage</h3>
              <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
                <code>
                  Â<sub>t</sub> &gt; 0
                </code>
              </div>
              <p>
                Increase the probability of the sampled token, but only within the clipped trust range.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="concept-card">
              <h3>Negative advantage</h3>
              <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
                <code>
                  Â<sub>t</sub> &lt; 0
                </code>
              </div>
              <p>
                Decrease the probability of the sampled token, again without letting the policy move too far.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="concept-card">
              <h3>KL penalty</h3>
              <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
                <code>
                  KL(π<sub>θ</sub> || π<sub>ref</sub>)
                </code>
              </div>
              <p>
                Keeps the updated policy close to the original reference model, preserving general language behavior.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3>7. Critic update: make value estimates less wrong</h3>

          <p>
            The critic is trained with a value loss. Its target is not the label itself; its target is a better prediction of
            expected future reward.
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              L<sub>value</sub> = (R<sub>t</sub> + γV<sub>t+1</sub> − V<sub>t</sub>)<sup>2</sup>
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            If the critic underestimates a prefix that later leads to a correct answer, this loss pushes its value upward.
            If it overestimates a prefix that leads to a wrong or malformed answer, the loss pushes its value downward.
          </p>

          <h3>8. One complete PPO iteration</h3>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Stage</th>
                  <th>What happens</th>
                  <th>What changes?</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Prepare</td>
                  <td>
                    Start from the current policy <code>π<sub>θk</sub></code>, a critic/value head, and a usually fixed
                    reference model <code>π<sub>ref</sub></code>.
                  </td>
                  <td>Nothing yet.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Rollout</td>
                  <td>
                    Generate responses using <code>π<sub>θk</sub></code>. Record sampled tokens, old log-probabilities, and
                    value estimates.
                  </td>
                  <td>Nothing yet.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Reward</td>
                  <td>
                    Extract the final answer and compare it with the ground-truth label or score.
                  </td>
                  <td>Reward is computed, but no model parameter changes.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Advantage</td>
                  <td>
                    Use critic values and rewards to compute <code>Â<sub>t</sub></code> backward through the response.
                  </td>
                  <td>Advantages are computed, but no model parameter changes.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Policy update</td>
                  <td>
                    Recompute current log-probabilities, form the ratio against old log-probabilities, and apply the clipped
                    PPO objective.
                  </td>
                  <td>LoRA policy parameters update.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Critic update</td>
                  <td>
                    Minimize the value loss so the critic becomes better at predicting future reward from a prefix.
                  </td>
                  <td>Value head / critic parameters update.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Repeat PPO epochs</td>
                  <td>
                    Reuse the same rollout batch for several PPO epochs. In this setup, each sampled batch is used for four
                    PPO epochs.
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    The current policy keeps changing, while the old log-probabilities from rollout stay fixed.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Next iteration</td>
                  <td>
                    Discard the old rollout batch. Generate a new batch with the updated policy.
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    <code>π<sub>θ(k+1)</sub></code> becomes the policy for the next rollout.
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">A concrete failure-to-success update</span>
            <p>
              Suppose the current model sees <em>This movie is wonderful</em> and generates
              <code>&lt;Judgement&gt;Negative&lt;/Judgement&gt;</code>. The extracted answer is wrong, so the reward is low.
              Tokens leading to <code>Negative</code> receive negative advantage, and PPO lowers their probability in similar
              states. If a later rollout generates <code>&lt;Judgement&gt;Positive&lt;/Judgement&gt;</code>, the reward is high,
              the relevant tokens receive positive advantage, and PPO raises their probability. Clipping prevents either
              update from turning into a large, unstable jump.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="rewards" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Reward design</h2>

          <p>
            The main paper intentionally uses simple rewards. This is part of the argument: even a basic task-correctness
            signal can turn a weak prompting model into a much stronger NLU model. But reward design is also the biggest
            limitation of the approach.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Task type</th>
                  <th>Reward</th>
                  <th>Format penalty</th>
                  <th>Intuition</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {rewardRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.task}</td>
                    <td>{row.reward}</td>
                    <td>{row.formatPenalty}</td>
                    <td>{row.intuition}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            For classification, the malformed-output penalty of <code>-1</code> is the strongest penalty in that reward
            scheme. A wrong but parseable answer receives <code>0</code>; an unparseable answer receives <code>-1</code>.
            This does not mean the paper uses a simple supervised loss equal to negative reward. PPO maximizes a clipped
            policy objective. The negative reward affects the advantage estimate, which pushes the model away from the
            token sequence that produced the malformed output.
          </p>

          <p>
            For <button class="anno" data-anno="stsb">STS-B</button>, the answer is a number from 0 to 5, so the paper
            defines reward as <code>2.5 - |ŷ - y|</code>. A perfect prediction receives <code>2.5</code>, the worst possible
            error receives <code>-2.5</code>, and malformed output receives the same lowest reward. This keeps the reward
            centered and gives PPO a graded signal rather than a binary correct/incorrect signal.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="lora" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Why LoRA</h2>

          <p>
            The paper uses <button class="anno" data-anno="lora">LoRA</button> for both SFT and PPO. The obvious reason
            is computational: full fine-tuning a 7B model is expensive, and PPO is already heavier than SFT because it
            requires generation, reward computation, advantage estimation, and policy updates.
          </p>

          <p>
            But LoRA also fits the paper's conceptual goal. The authors are not trying to overwrite the model's language
            knowledge. They assume the pretrained LLM already contains useful syntax, semantics, and world knowledge.
            The adaptation problem is to steer output behavior toward NLU labels. Freezing the backbone and training
            low-rank updates lets the model move in a smaller parameter subspace.
          </p>

          <div class="architecture-flow">
            <span>Frozen LLAMA2 backbone</span>
            <span>+</span>
            <span>Trainable low-rank LoRA updates</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Task-adapted generation policy</span>
          </div>

          <p>
            This is also why LoRA is more natural here than simply adding a classification head. The paper wants the
            LLM to keep answering through generation, because PPO treats the generated tokens as actions. A separate
            classification head would change the interface into a discriminative classifier. LoRA changes the generation
            distribution while preserving the model's normal prompting interface.
          </p>
        </section>

