        <section id="reward-model" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Reward model experiment</h2>

          <p>
            This experiment asks what happens when the reward is no longer just exact label matching. The main PPO setup
            rewards the final label: correct, wrong, or malformed. That works well for benchmark classification, but many
            language-understanding tasks also care about explanation quality, consistency, factuality, and helpfulness.
            A <button class="anno" data-anno="rewardModel">reward model</button> is one way to turn those softer qualities
            into a scalar reward that PPO can optimize.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Component</th>
                  <th>What happens</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {rewardModelRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.component}</td>
                    <td>{row.detail}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h3>From label reward to learned reward</h3>

          <p>
            In the simple reward setup, PPO only needs to check whether the extracted label matches the ground truth.
            The reward is direct and task-specific:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              R = 1(ŷ = y)
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            The reward-model setup changes the source of the reward. The LLM generates both a sentiment judgement and an
            analysis. Instead of assigning reward only by exact label match, a separate model scores the whole response:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              r<sub>θ</sub>(x, y)
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            Here, <code>x</code> is the input example, <code>y</code> is the generated response, and
            <code>r<sub>θ</sub>(x, y)</code> is the reward model's scalar score for that response. PPO can then use this score
            as the reward signal.
          </p>

          <h3>How the reward model learns preferences</h3>

          <p>
            The reward model is trained with pairwise ranking. For the same input, a better response should receive a higher
            score than a worse response. The training data therefore contains pairs:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              (x, y<sub>w</sub>, y<sub>l</sub>)
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            <code>y<sub>w</sub></code> is the winner, or the higher-ranked response. <code>y<sub>l</sub></code> is the loser,
            or the lower-ranked response. The desired inequality is:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              r<sub>θ</sub>(x, y<sub>w</sub>) &gt; r<sub>θ</sub>(x, y<sub>l</sub>)
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            The loss turns that preference into a differentiable objective:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              L(θ) = −E<sub>(x,y<sub>w</sub>,y<sub>l</sub>)∼D</sub>
              [log σ(r<sub>θ</sub>(x,y<sub>w</sub>) − r<sub>θ</sub>(x,y<sub>l</sub>))]
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            The inside of the sigmoid is the score gap between the preferred and less preferred response:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              Δr = r<sub>θ</sub>(x,y<sub>w</sub>) − r<sub>θ</sub>(x,y<sub>l</sub>)
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            If the reward model already gives the better response a much higher score, <code>Δr</code> is large,
            <code>σ(Δr)</code> is close to <code>1</code>, and the loss is small. If the worse response receives a higher
            score, <code>Δr</code> becomes negative, <code>σ(Δr)</code> becomes small, and the loss becomes large. Training
            therefore pushes the reward model to rank better answers above worse ones.
          </p>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">What counts as better?</span>
            <p>
              Responses are ranked not only by whether the sentiment label is correct, but also by whether the explanation is
              consistent with the label, factually reasonable, and helpful. The intended preference order is:
              <code>correct answer with analysis</code> &gt; <code>only correct answer</code> &gt;
              <code>incorrect answer with analysis</code> &gt; <code>only incorrect answer</code>.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3>How it changes PPO</h3>

          <p>
            With exact label matching, PPO receives a simple task reward. With the learned reward model, PPO receives a score
            from <code>r<sub>θ</sub>(x, y)</code>. The rest of the PPO machinery is the same: the generated response receives
            a reward, advantages are computed, and the policy is updated to make high-reward responses more likely.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Reward source</th>
                  <th>What PPO is pushed toward</th>
                  <th>Main trade-off</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Exact label reward</td>
                  <td>Output the correct class label in the required format.</td>
                  <td>Strong classification accuracy, but little pressure to improve explanation quality.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Learned reward model</td>
                  <td>Output a response that looks better under the learned preference scorer.</td>
                  <td>Better analyses, but the reward may become less directly aligned with classification accuracy.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h3>Why the result matters</h3>

          <p>
            The result is a useful warning. PPO-RM improves generated analysis quality, but reduces SST-2 accuracy. That does
            not mean PPO itself failed. It means the reward changed the target. Exact label reward asks the model to be sharp
            at classification. A learned reward model asks for a broader behavior: correct judgement, coherent explanation,
            factuality, and helpfulness.
          </p>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">The lesson</span>
            <p>
              PPO is an optimizer; the reward is the specification. If the reward model is trained on limited data, or if the
              policy finds ways to satisfy the reward model without improving the underlying task, the model can trade away
              accuracy for outputs that look better to the scorer.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            This is why reward design is not a side detail. For simple NLU benchmarks, exact label matching is direct and
            reliable. For richer language-understanding tasks, the reward must represent a more complex target, and that
            target must be learned carefully enough that PPO does not optimize the wrong behavior.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="limitations" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Limitations</h2>

          <p>
            The main limitation is that the paper's strongest results still rely on ground-truth labels. Even though the
            training is framed as reinforcement learning, the reward often comes from exact comparison with the labeled
            answer. That is different from SFT, but it is not fully annotation-free.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second limitation is that simple rewards fit structured benchmarks better than open-ended understanding.
            GLUE and SuperGLUE are good for this paper because many tasks have clear labels or automatic metrics. Real
            language understanding may involve ambiguous answers, long reasoning, factual support, or multiple valid
            explanations. These cases need more robust reward models.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third limitation is output format dependence. The model is rewarded after its answer is extracted from a
            fixed structure. That is practical and necessary for automatic scoring, but it means the method depends on a
            reliable interface between free-form generation and discrete task evaluation.
          </p>

          <p>
            The paper's own conclusion is therefore balanced: PPO is a promising way to adapt already pretrained LLMs to
            NLU tasks, but the long-term research problem is not simply "use PPO." It is how to define environments and
            rewards that are reliable, generalizable, and resistant to reward hacking.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section
          id="experiment-design"
          class="reading-section"
          style="border-left: 4px solid var(--accent, #3b82f6); padding-left: 1.25rem;"
        >
          <h2>How to design convincing experiments</h2>

          <p>
            A strong empirical paper is not just a proposed method followed by a few scores. The experiments have to build a
            chain of evidence. They should prove the main claim, compare against fair alternatives, explain why the method works,
            test whether the gain generalizes, report the cost, and show the boundary cases. This is why the experimental section
            here feels unusually complete: it does not only say that PPO wins on one benchmark; it tests the claim from several
            angles.
          </p>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">The core idea</span>
            <p>
              A good experiment section should make a skeptical reader think: the method beats reasonable alternatives, the
              improvement is not accidental, the cost is understandable, and the limitations are visible.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Evidence needed</th>
                  <th>Question to answer</th>
                  <th>How this study answers it</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Main result</td>
                  <td>
                    Does the method directly support the central claim?
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    GLUE is used as the main benchmark because the claim is about improving NLU. PPO is compared against
                    prompting, SFT, and BERT-style encoder models on standard NLU tasks.
                  </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                  <td>Fair baselines</td>
                  <td>
                    Is the method compared against alternatives that a reader would actually consider?
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    The baselines include zero-shot prompting, few-shot prompting, SFT, BERT-base, and BERT-large. This makes
                    the comparison stronger than only showing that PPO beats a weak prompt.
                  </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                  <td>Mechanism</td>
                  <td>
                    Why does the method help?
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    PPO is compared with SFT to test whether reward-driven optimization adds something beyond supervised
                    next-token training. PPO is also compared with GRPO to ask whether the gain is specific to PPO or more
                    broadly related to RL-style optimization.
                  </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                  <td>Stress test</td>
                  <td>
                    Does the method still work when the benchmark is harder?
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    SuperGLUE is used as a harder NLU benchmark. This checks whether the result is more than a GLUE-specific
                    improvement.
                  </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                  <td>Cross-model evidence</td>
                  <td>
                    Is the method tied to one model family?
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    The experiments include LLAMA2, Qwen2.5, and MPT. This makes the argument less dependent on one particular
                    checkpoint.
                  </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                  <td>Generalization</td>
                  <td>
                    Does the model only learn the training dataset, or does the behavior transfer?
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    Models fine-tuned on SST-2 are tested on other sentiment datasets, and models fine-tuned on MNLI are tested
                    on other NLI datasets. This checks whether the learned behavior survives outside the original training task.
                  </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                  <td>Cost</td>
                  <td>
                    What is the practical price of the improvement?
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    LoRA reduces trainable parameters. PPO and GRPO are compared by runtime, and the study also notes that
                    LLAMA-style inference is much slower than BERT-style inference.
                  </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                  <td>Side effects</td>
                  <td>
                    Does fine-tuning damage other useful abilities?
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    Instruction-following is tested with an out-of-distribution Amazon review task, and language modeling
                    ability is checked with WikiText-2 perplexity.
                  </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                  <td>Failure or trade-off</td>
                  <td>
                    Where does the method become less straightforward?
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    The reward model experiment improves generated analysis quality but lowers SST-2 accuracy. This shows that
                    richer rewards can change the target and introduce trade-offs.
                  </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                  <td>Extension</td>
                  <td>
                    Can the same idea apply beyond classification?
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    The SQuAD experiment uses Exact Match and F1 as reward signals, showing how PPO can be extended to reading
                    comprehension.
                  </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                  <td>Starting point</td>
                  <td>
                    Does the initialization matter?
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    The comparison between LLaMA2-base and LLaMA2-chat shows that an instruction-tuned starting point is more
                    suitable for this kind of task-specific RL adaptation.
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            This is the difference between a narrow benchmark win and a convincing empirical argument. A narrow version would
            simply say: <em>PPO improves GLUE.</em> A stronger version says: PPO improves the main benchmark, remains competitive
            on a harder benchmark, works across several model families, transfers to related datasets, has measurable cost, keeps
            more of the original model's behavior than SFT, and reveals an important trade-off when the reward becomes more
            complex.
          </p>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">A reusable checklist</span>
            <p>
              When reading or designing a method paper, ask: What is the main claim? Are the baselines fair? What explains the
              gain? Where else does it work? What does it cost? What abilities might it damage? Where does it fail or trade off?
              If the experiments answer these questions, the paper is no longer just reporting numbers; it is making a case.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

